2. Student Responsibilities
Students are responsible for:
attending scheduled classes regularly;
arriving at classes and activities on time;
completing assignments before the stated deadlines;
preparing for lessons, examinations, and academic activities;
following instructions provided by lecturers and staff;
maintaining academic honesty and personal integrity;
treating other students, lecturers, staff, and visitors respectfully;
protecting institutional property and learning resources;
keeping their personal and contact information updated;
checking official announcements and academic notices regularly.
Students who do not understand a requirement should ask a lecturer, academic advisor, or Student Affairs representative for clarification.

3. Attendance
Regular attendance is important for academic success.
Students should attend all scheduled:
lectures;
tutorials;
laboratory sessions;
examinations;
workshops;
group activities;
required academic events.
Students who are unable to attend a class should inform the lecturer as early as possible.
An absence may be considered excused when it is caused by:
illness;
a family emergency;
an approved institutional activity;
another reason accepted by the institution.
Students may be required to provide supporting documentation for an extended absence.
Missing classes does not remove a student’s responsibility to complete assignments, review missed lessons, or meet academic deadlines.

4. Punctuality
Students should arrive before each class begins.
Students who arrive late should:
enter the classroom quietly;
avoid interrupting the lecturer or other students;
follow any attendance instructions;
speak with the lecturer after class when an explanation is required.
Repeated lateness may be reported to the academic advisor or Student Affairs Office.

5. Classroom Conduct
Students should help maintain a productive learning environment.
During class, students should:
listen when another person is speaking;
participate respectfully in discussions;
follow the lecturer’s instructions;
use appropriate language;
keep mobile phones on silent mode;
avoid unnecessary conversations;
avoid actions that distract other students;
use laptops and mobile devices only for approved learning purposes;
respect different opinions and learning styles.
Students must not engage in bullying, harassment, discrimination, intimidation, or threatening behavior.

6. Academic Integrity
Academic integrity means completing academic work honestly and responsibly.
Students must not:
copy another student’s assignment;
allow another person to complete their work;
use unauthorized notes or devices during an examination;
submit work obtained from the internet as their own;
invent research data or references;
change academic records dishonestly;
help another student cheat;
submit the same assignment for multiple courses without approval.
When using information from books, websites, articles, reports, or other sources, students must provide proper citations.
Suspected academic misconduct may be reviewed by the lecturer, department, or academic committee.
Possible outcomes may include:
a warning;
resubmission of the assignment;
a reduced grade;
a score of zero;
failure of the course;
suspension;
another disciplinary action permitted by institutional policy.

7. Assignment Submission
Students must submit assignments through the method specified by the lecturer.
Submission methods may include:
the online learning platform;
email;
printed submission;
departmental submission;
another approved method.
Students should confirm that:
the correct file was submitted;
the file can be opened;
the student’s name and identification number are included;
all required sections are complete;
the assignment was submitted before the deadline.
A technical problem should be reported immediately. Students should not wait until after the deadline to report a known issue.
Late assignments may receive a penalty unless an extension has been approved.

8. Examination Procedures
Students must follow examination instructions carefully.
Before an examination, students should:
review course materials;
confirm the examination date, time, and location;
arrive at least 20 minutes early;
bring their student identification card;
bring approved stationery and materials;
leave prohibited items outside the examination area.
During an examination, students must:
sit in the assigned seat when required;
follow the invigilator’s instructions;
keep mobile phones switched off;
avoid communicating with other students;
use only authorized materials;
remain quiet;
stop writing when instructed;
submit all required examination materials before leaving.
Students who arrive late may be allowed to enter only according to examination rules. Additional time may not be provided.
Students who become ill during an examination should inform the invigilator immediately.

9. Use of Technology
Institutional computers, internet services, online platforms, and digital resources must be used responsibly.
Students must:
protect their usernames and passwords;
avoid sharing accounts;
use institutional systems for lawful purposes;
respect copyright and intellectual property rights;
avoid accessing harmful or inappropriate content;
avoid installing unauthorized software;
avoid attempting to access another user’s account;
report suspected security problems.
Students are responsible for activities performed through their accounts unless unauthorized access is reported promptly.

10. Library Use
Students are encouraged to use the library for study and research.
Students should:
maintain a quiet environment;
return borrowed materials on time;
handle books and equipment carefully;
follow computer and study-area rules;
avoid removing materials without authorization;
pay applicable replacement costs for lost or damaged items.
Food and drinks may be restricted in certain library areas.

11. Health and Safety
Students must follow all campus health and safety instructions.
Students should:
participate in emergency drills;
learn the location of emergency exits;
report accidents and injuries immediately;
report damaged or unsafe equipment;
keep hallways and exits clear;
follow laboratory safety instructions;
avoid bringing dangerous or prohibited items onto campus;
follow instructions given by security or emergency personnel.
In an emergency, students should remain calm and follow official instructions.

12. Respect for Institutional Property
Students must use classrooms, laboratories, furniture, computers, sports equipment, and other institutional property responsibly.
Students must not:
intentionally damage property;
write on walls, desks, or equipment;
remove institutional property without permission;
misuse laboratory or technical equipment;
interfere with safety equipment;
access restricted areas without authorization.
Students may be required to pay for damage caused intentionally or through negligence.

13. Student Support Services
Students who need assistance are encouraged to seek support early.
Available support may include:
academic advising;
counseling services;
library assistance;
disability support;
career guidance;
financial aid information;
technology support;
health services;
peer tutoring;
student clubs and activities.
Students experiencing academic difficulties should contact their lecturer or academic advisor.
Students experiencing personal or emotional difficulties may contact the counseling or Student Affairs Office.

14. Complaints and Appeals
Students may raise concerns about academic decisions, services, facilities, staff conduct, or student treatment.
A student should normally:
discuss the concern with the relevant lecturer or staff member;
contact the department or Student Affairs Office if the concern is unresolved;
submit a formal written complaint when required;
provide relevant evidence and supporting information;
follow the appeal procedure if dissatisfied with the decision.
Complaints should be submitted honestly and respectfully.
The institution should handle complaints fairly and protect confidential information where possible.

15. Disciplinary Actions
Students who violate institutional rules may face disciplinary action depending on the seriousness and frequency of the behavior.
Possible actions include:
verbal warning;
written warning;
required counseling;
community service;
restriction from certain facilities;
removal from an activity;
suspension from classes;
suspension from the institution;
expulsion.
Before serious disciplinary action is imposed, the student should be informed of the concern and given an opportunity to respond according to institutional procedures.

16. Student Commitment
By enrolling in the institution, students agree to:
follow institutional policies and procedures;
maintain academic honesty;
respect other members of the community;
attend classes and complete academic responsibilities;
use institutional resources responsibly;
contribute to a safe and inclusive environment;
represent the institution professionally.
